menu "BuddyOS display firmware"

config BUDDY_NET
    bool "Bring up Wi-Fi and MQTT"
    default y
    help
        Off gives the face alone, which is the baseline the memory figures are
        measured against. On is the number the planning docs actually ask for.

config BUDDY_WIFI_SSID
    string "Bench Wi-Fi SSID (used only when the kit was never set up)"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""
    help
        A shipped kit is set up over BLE from the website, and what it is told
        there is kept in NVS and always wins. This and the other bench settings
        below are the laptop path: used only when NVS holds nothing, and ignored
        after BOOT has been held for 5 s (see main/prov.h). Empty, an unprovisioned
        kit boots straight into BLE setup.

config BUDDY_WIFI_PASS
    string "Wi-Fi password"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_MQTT_URI
    string "MQTT broker URI"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""
    help
        Empty by default, and empty is the right answer for a kit that is set up
        from the website: setup tells it where to go. Set this only for a bench
        build that is never provisioned — e.g. mqtts://broker.example.com:8883.
        Topics are namespaced by environment rather than by broker, see
        BUDDY_MQTT_ENV.
        mqtt:// is plaintext. mqtts:// pulls in mbedTLS, whose handshake is the
        expensive moment — and on this product line a starved handshake has already
        been seen to misreport itself as NOT_TRUSTED rather than as out of memory.

config BUDDY_MQTT_USER
    string "Bench serial (MQTT username) for the bench token"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_MQTT_PASS
    string "Bench MQTT password (the device JWT)"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""
    help
        The token ../user-service-viettel mints at POST /v1/mqtt/generate-token. It
        carries the EMQX ACL, so which topics this device may touch is decided by
        the token rather than by anything here.

config BUDDY_MQTT_ENV
    string "Bench topic namespace (the stem-gateway environment)"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default "staging"
    help
        Topics are {env}/stem/device/{username}/{directive,event,log}. It must match
        the `environment` claim in the token or every publish is denied.

config BUDDY_GATEWAY_HOST
    string "Bench stem-gateway host"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""
    help
        Host only, and empty by default. A kit set up from the website is TOLD its
        gateway during setup — the website sends the one matching its own
        environment — so this is only the bench fallback for a build that is never
        provisioned. With it empty, a kit that was never set up simply does not
        start the voice socket and says so in the log.
        The voice socket dials wss://<host>/v1/stem/voice/stream.

config BUDDY_SLEEP
    bool "Sleep when idle, and on a device.sleep directive"
    default y
    help
        Deep sleep, woken by a press of the button below. The kit blanks its panel
        and stops listening; only the button brings it back.

        See docs: display-kit-sleep-mode in the planning repo.

config BUDDY_SLEEP_IDLE_S
    int "Seconds of quiet before the kit sleeps (0 disables)"
    depends on BUDDY_SLEEP
    range 0 3600
    default 180
    help
        Counted from the last thing the kit did or was told: a wake word, a
        directive, a button, a conversation. NOT the same timer as
        BUDDY_VOICE_IDLE_S, which ends a *conversation* after 15 s and leaves the
        kit awake and listening. This one ends the *kit*.

        **After this, the wake word stops working** — deep sleep has no microphone,
        so the button is the only way back. 0 disables idle sleep entirely, which is
        what a classroom or a demo table wants.

config BUDDY_SLEEP_WAKE_GPIO
    int "GPIO that wakes the kit (RTC GPIO; -1 on the P4 for RESET only)"
    depends on BUDDY_SLEEP
    range 0 21 if !IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    range -1 15 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default 0 if !IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default -1
    help
        ESP32-P4: waking from deep sleep needs an LP GPIO (0-15), through ext1. The
        Waveshare board's BOOT button is GPIO 35, which is not one, and nothing else
        on the board is a button — so the default is -1, no wake pin, and the kit's
        RESET button wakes it. Waking is a cold boot either way, so RESET loses
        nothing a wake pin would have kept. Wire a button from an LP GPIO to GND and
        name it here to have one.

        Must be an **RTC GPIO**: waking from deep sleep uses ext0, which the ESP32-S3
        supports only on GPIO 0-21 (SOC_RTCIO_PIN_COUNT is 22). GPIO 47 cannot do it
        at any speed.

        0 is the BOOT button, present on every S3 devkit and already read by this
        firmware for the 5-second factory reset, so a short press and a long hold are
        two gestures on one button.

        NOTE: GPIO 0 is also a strapping pin — held low at reset it selects download
        mode. A press-and-release to wake should be clear of the latch, but this has
        not been confirmed on hardware.

        On the ST7701S board GPIO 0 is ALSO DB17 of the panel bus. It still works
        here because the panel is dark by the time ext0 is armed and sleep_now()
        takes the pin back first. There is no fallback any more: every RTC GPIO is
        in use — 0-18 by the panel and audio, 19/20 by native USB, 21 by PCLK. If
        BOOT misbehaves as a wake pin, the answer is a different panel pin-out, not
        a different number here.

config BUDDY_BOOT_RESET
    bool "Hold BOOT (GPIO 0) for 5 s to erase setup and re-enter BLE setup"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default y
    help
        On the ST7701S board BOOT is DB17 of the panel bus and cannot be read while
        the panel runs, so it is read once at boot, before the panel starts. A cold
        reset with BOOT held is the ROM's download mode, so in practice the gesture
        is: while the kit SLEEPS, press BOOT and keep holding for 5 s. An awake kit
        has to sleep first (a device.sleep directive, or the idle timer).

config BUDDY_BOOT_BUTTON_SETUP
    bool "Press BOOT while running to set the kit up again"
    depends on BUDDY_NET && IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default y
    help
        The boot-time gesture above cannot be reached on this board, so this is the
        one that works here: while the kit runs, hold BOOT, then let go.

        BUDDY_BOOT_RESET reads the pin once, as the first thing app_main does, which
        means something must already be holding it down by then. On the S3 that is a
        press that woke the kit from sleep. The P4's BOOT is GPIO 35: not an LP GPIO,
        so it cannot wake the chip, and holding it through a reset lands in the ROM's
        download mode rather than in the firmware. No sequence reaches the check.

        GPIO 35 is BOOT and nothing else on this board, so unlike the S3's GPIO 0 —
        which is a panel data line while the display runs — it is safe to read at
        any time.

config BUDDY_BOOT_BUTTON_MS
    int "...how long BOOT must be held, ms"
    depends on BUDDY_BOOT_BUTTON_SETUP
    range 500 10000
    default 2000
    help
        Long enough that a knock cannot wipe a kit, short enough to count out loud.
        The erase happens on RELEASE, so letting go early is how you change your mind.

config BUDDY_MQTT_AWS_ROOT
    bool "Verify the broker with the embedded Amazon Root CA 1"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default y
    help
        The product broker sits behind an AWS certificate, and one root is 1.2 KB
        against the full bundle's 65 KB. Turn this off to reach a broker with some
        other publicly-trusted certificate, which costs the whole bundle.

choice BUDDY_SCREEN
    prompt "What the panel shows"
    default BUDDY_SCREEN_FACE
    help
        The face is the product; the gallery is the football demo. They are exclusive
        because each wants the whole panel, and because only one of them can own the
        mouth that lip-sync drives.

config BUDDY_SCREEN_FACE
    bool "The Buddy's face"
    help
        Sprites composited per layer - face, brows, eyes, mouth - with eyes and brows
        animating from the authored motion while the mouth follows the audio being
        spoken. Costs what the artwork costs: ARGB8888 baked at the panel's own size,
        about 4 MB at 720x720, which is why the P4 carries a 32 MB partition table.

config BUDDY_SCREEN_GALLERY
    bool "A photo gallery"
    help
        Photos swiped through by touch, each able to drop its jaw if gallery/mouths.csv
        says where its mouth is. No eyes: a photograph's cannot move.

endchoice

config BUDDY_FACE_MOTION_DEMO
    bool "Cycle the website's whole-head motion presets"
    depends on BUDDY_SCREEN_FACE
    default n
    help
        Plays nod, bounce, shake and breathe on the head in turn, six seconds each,
        naming each one on the console as it starts. For looking at what the panel
        can do with each: on the P4 the body is composited by the PPA, which
        translates exactly but cannot rotate by less than a quarter turn or scale by
        less than a sixteenth - so nod and bounce move the body and shake and
        breathe move only the features. A fifth preset, shake-x, is the same rhythm
        as a translation, which is what a head shake can be on this hardware.

        A demo, not a product behaviour: it talks over the state machine's own
        motion, so leave it off unless that is what you want to see.

config BUDDY_AEC_NLP_LEVEL
    int "Echo suppression (AEC NLP): 0 normal, 1 aggressive, 2 very aggressive"
    depends on BUDDY_AEC
    range 0 2
    default 1
    help
        How hard the echo canceller suppresses what the linear filter leaves behind —
        which, on a full-duplex kit, is the Buddy's own voice arriving back through
        its microphones. Too little and the far end hears the kit and takes it for
        the child, so the Buddy interrupts itself mid-sentence.

        It is a knob rather than a constant because the two failures it sits between
        are both real on this board, and which one bites depends on the enclosure,
        the volume and the room:

        0 (normal) - the kit sometimes hears its own speaker.
        1 (aggressive) - the ESP-SR default, and the default here.
        2 (very aggressive) - the strongest suppression available.

        The cost of turning it up: NLP suppresses whoever is *quieter* during
        playback, and that is the child as well as the speaker. This board's
        microphone delivers speech at about -47 dBFS where voice.c reckons arm's
        length to be -25, so WakeNet already runs near its threshold, and turning
        suppression up is what made the wake word intermittent when this was last
        measured. If the kit stops waking, come back down. The real fix is level,
        not suppression - see the mic gain note in voice.c.


config BUDDY_AUDIO
    bool "Bring up the microphone and amplifier"
    depends on !BUDDY_NET || BUDDY_NET
    default n
    help
        Off by default until the audio board has been brought up once by hand.

        The first attempt hung the device hard enough that the ROM bootloader stopped
        answering — recovery needed the BOOT button — so this stays opt-in rather than
        running on every boot of a device that is otherwise working.

config BUDDY_AUDIO_SELFTEST
    bool "Play a tone and report what the microphone hears, at boot"
    depends on BUDDY_AUDIO
    default n
    help
        Useful exactly once per board, to prove the wiring. It drives the amplifier,
        which on USB power can sag the rail on a device already running a panel and a
        radio — so it is not something to leave on.

config BUDDY_VOICE
    bool "Talk to the voice assistant"
    depends on BUDDY_NET && BUDDY_AUDIO
    default n
    help
        Streams the microphone to the gateway's voice route as Opus and plays the
        Buddy's replies. See main/voice.c.

config BUDDY_VOICE_TOKEN
    string "Bench user token the voice route resolves the persona with"
    depends on BUDDY_VOICE
    default ""
    help
        A user's JWT. omni resolves the kit's persona and voice from BYA with it.
        A kit set up over BLE uses the user_jwt it was given there instead; this
        is the bench fallback, kept in sdkconfig, which is gitignored.

config BUDDY_WAKE_WORD
    bool "Start a conversation on a wake word"
    depends on BUDDY_VOICE
    default y
    help
        The microphone stays on the device, feeding ESP-SR WakeNet, until it hears
        the wake word chosen under "ESP Speech Recognition" (Jarvis by default).
        Only then does the kit open its voice session, and it closes it again after
        BUDDY_VOICE_IDLE_S of quiet. Off: the session opens at boot and never
        closes, which is how the pipeline was first brought up.

config BUDDY_UPLINK_GAIN_DB
    int "Digital gain on the audio sent to the cloud, dB"
    depends on BUDDY_VOICE
    range 0 24
    default 0
    help
        Applied to the microphone AFTER the front end and BEFORE the Opus encoder, so
        it lifts only what the cloud hears. WakeNet is not affected: it runs inside
        the AFE, upstream of this.

        It exists because this kit is quiet. Measured on the bench, speech at
        conversational distance reaches the AFE at about -47 dBFS peak, where
        voice.c's own note puts arm's length at -25, and a browser client arrives
        near -20. Everything in the cloud is tuned for that: omni's speech detector
        gates onsets on absolute loudness, so a kit 20 dB under simply never
        registers as speaking and cannot interrupt a reply, however clean its audio.

        Raising the ES7210's analogue gain is NOT the way to do this. Tried at 37 dB
        on the bench, it lifted the playback reference slot some twenty times rather
        than the microphones, and AEC then cancelled against an echo that was not
        there and deafened the wake word entirely.

        0 disables it, and 0 is the default BECAUSE the one thing it was built for
        turned out not to need it. Raised to 18 dB on the bench, omni's ASR went on
        transcribing the kit at 0.85 confidence exactly as it had before — the audio
        was never too quiet for the cloud to hear — and Silero still never reported a
        single speech onset in 114 seconds (vad_speech_active=False), so barge-in did
        not move. Whatever stops that VAD firing is not level. Peaks sit ~30 dB below
        full scale, so 18 does not clip if you do need it for something else.

config BUDDY_VOICE_IDLE_S
    int "Seconds of quiet that end a conversation"
    depends on BUDDY_WAKE_WORD
    range 5 120
    default 15
    help
        Counted from the last thing that happened: the child's voice at the mic,
        or any state change from omni. A reply that is still playing is not quiet.

config BUDDY_WAKE_THRESHOLD_MODE
    int "WakeNet detection mode (0 = normal, 1 = aggressive)"
    depends on BUDDY_WAKE_WORD
    range 0 1
    default 1
    help
        ESP-SR's DET_MODE_90 / DET_MODE_95. Aggressive wakes more readily, with
        more false wakes; with a single, cheap, far-field INMP441 and no AFE in
        front of it, normal mode misses too many.

config BUDDY_VOICE_TAP
    bool "Copy every uplink Opus packet to the console"
    depends on BUDDY_VOICE
    default n
    help
        A diagnostic. Prints the exact packets sent to omni as base64 lines,
        "TAP <frame> <base64>", while the child is speaking — about 3 KB/s, which
        even the UART console carries. tools/voice_tap.py turns them back into an
        Ogg Opus file of exactly what omni heard, which can be listened to and
        replayed to omni, so a speech recognition problem can be reproduced
        offline without anyone having to repeat themselves. Off in any build
        that ships.

config BUDDY_SPEAKER_VOLUME
    int "Speaker volume (ESP32-P4 board), percent"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    range 0 100
    default 75
    help
        The ES8311's output volume. The S3 board's amplifier has no volume control;
        this one does.

config BUDDY_MIC_GAIN_DB
    int "Microphone gain (ESP32-P4 board), dB"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    range 0 37
    default 30
    help
        The ES7210's analogue gain on every microphone slot. A loopback slot ('R' in
        BUDDY_AFE_INPUT_FORMAT) is left at 0 dB: it carries the codec's own output,
        which is already full scale.

config BUDDY_AFE_INPUT_FORMAT
    string "ES7210 slot layout (ESP32-P4 board)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default "MRMN"
    help
        What each of the ES7210's four TDM slots carries, one letter each, in ESP-SR's
        notation: M a microphone, R the playback loopback (the echo reference), N
        nothing. The first M is the one audio_read() returns. If no slot is R, the
        echo reference is made in software from what the firmware plays, delayed by
        the I2S queue.

        audio_selftest() prints each slot's level in silence and under a test tone.
        A loopback slot reads near-digital silence until the tone and then far louder
        than either microphone; a slot that never moves is N.

config BUDDY_AEC
    bool "Acoustic echo cancellation (ESP-SR AFE)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4 && BUDDY_WAKE_WORD
    default y
    help
        Feed the microphones and the echo reference through ESP-SR's audio front end:
        echo cancellation, then blind source separation across the two microphones,
        then WakeNet. With it, the kit can hear "Jarvis" — and the child — while the
        Buddy is talking, so the microphone is no longer muted during playback.
        Off: the S3 behaviour, one microphone and half duplex.

config BUDDY_AEC_KEEP_HALF_DUPLEX
    bool "Still mute the upstream audio while the Buddy speaks"
    depends on BUDDY_AEC
    default n
    help
        Echo cancellation leaves a residue, and the cloud's own voice activity
        detection may hear it as the child talking. If the Buddy keeps interrupting
        itself, turn this on: the wake word still works during playback (barge-in),
        but nothing is streamed until the Buddy is done.

config BUDDY_LIPSYNC_LEAD_MS
    int "Lip-sync: how far behind the codec the speaker is (ms)"
    range 0 1000
    default 100
    help
        The photos' mouths follow the Buddy's speech, classified as the PCM is
        handed to the codec, and that PCM is heard a DMA queue later. Each mouth
        shape is shown this long after it was classified. Too small and the mouth
        moves before the sound; too large and after. Measure it on the board:
        film the screen and the speaker together and count frames.

config BUDDY_LIPSYNC_DEMO
    bool "Lip-sync: babble without a voice session (bench only)"
    default n
    help
        The current photo talks all the time, from a fixed pattern of mouth
        shapes, so the jaw can be judged without starting a conversation. Off in
        any build that ships.

endmenu
